\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Introduction: \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 What would it be to have a distinctively "biblical" Method of Reading/Interpreting the Bible?  \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 In our introductory session, we discussed how the Bible is fundamentally a \ldblquote narrative.\rdblquote  \par  \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\faauto\ls32\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 How then is the narrative organized?  \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}What is the internal \ldblquote logic\rdblquote  of the Bible that drives the narrative?   \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}What is \ldblquote progressing\rdblquote  throughout the Bible?  \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par To answer these questions is to locate from the Bible itself the inherent logic that would then drive our method of interpretation.   \par \par In this session, we will discover that the inherent internal organization of the Bible is \ldblquote Covenant\rdblquote  even as then to necessitate a covenantal methodology in interpreting the Bible! \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1. What is a \ldblquote Covenant\rdblquote \par \par A. The Redemptive-Historical Context Of The Bible in Relation to Covenant\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 c.f. G. Vos's }{\i\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Biblical Theology}{\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   (one of our recommended readings) \par \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1. Biblical Theology:  "deals with the process of the self-revelation of God deposited in the Bible."  G. Vos\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 I.e. God's speaks, we listen-- truth is from God alone as relevant to God's causing redemption. \par \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2. Main Features of a Redemptive Historical Understanding of the Bible:\par      a. The historic progressiveness of the revelation-process. \par }\pard \ql \li1340\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1340\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Revelation is the interpretation of redemption; it must, therefore, unfold itself in installments as redemption does.  (Vos. p.6)\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525      b. The actual embodiment of revelation in history.\par \tab "the facts of history themselves acquire a revealing significance."(Vos. p.6)\par \tab Read p.6-7\par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "The usual order is: first word, then the fact, then again the interpretive word."\par Example: "The Old Testament brings the predictive preparatory word, the Gospels record the redemptive-revelatory fact, the Epistles supply the subsequent, final interpretation." (p.7) \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525      c. The organic nature of the historic process is observable in revelation.\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 I.e. From seed form to full growth, qualitatively, the seed is not less perfect than the tree.  We should therefore read the Bible as within an expectation that there is not multiple theologies presented within it, but rather one theology as becoming more and more clear as redemptive history progresses.  We have license then to understand the "theological vision" of an earlier portion by it's relevant to a later portion.\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525      d. The practical adaptability of Biblical Theology.\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "The Biblical idea (of "to know") is to have the reality of something practically interwoven with the inner experience of life." (Vos, p.8)\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 3. The Covenantal Nature of Redemptive History\par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 i.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-180\li1080\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl2\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 A gracious condescension by God }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (WCF 7.1)}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The distance between God and the creature is so great, that although reasonable creatures do owe obedience unto Him as their Creator, yet they could never have any fruition of Him as their blessedness and reward, but by some voluntary condescension on God's part, which He hath been pleased to express by way of covenant.\~\par \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 ii.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-180\li1080\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl2\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gracious Condescension vis-\u224\'88-vis ancient Near Eastern (Hittite) Treaty: (example,  Gen-Exodus and Deut.) c.f. Meredith Kline, \ldblquote What is a Covenant\rdblquote  and Addendum \ldblquote Covenant Treaty Format, Ancient Near Eastern Kingdoms.\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Observation: We see the "covenantal" nature of Biblical revelation if by the mere fact that the Hebrew word for covenant ("berith") is used 289 times in the Old Testament, The "berith" language is used explicitly to summarize the Genesis histories in Exodus}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2:24, God heard their groaning, and God remembered his }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 covenant }{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.   Clearly God was acting in covenant through Moses as noted in Exodus 24:7 Then he took the book of the }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 covenant,}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  and read it in the hearing of the people; and they said, \ldblquote All that the LORD has spoken we will do, and we will be obedient.\rdblquote   }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 8}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Moses took the blood and dashed it on the people, and said, \ldblquote See the blood of the covenant that the LORD has made with you in accordance with all these words.\rdblquote    As we will see, even the prophets themselves where those appointed by God as the covenant executors, to proclaim the terms of the covenant as especially related to the curses and blessings attached to them-- this will account for the prophets constantly applying the curses of Deuteronomy to the sufferings of the Israel people and the hope for blessings to the future Israel. \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li810\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin810\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Just as the "covenant" framework can be noted in a covenant renewal book like Deuteronomy, so we see this framework in the Bible as a whole in successive "berith" making contexts. \par \par So, for instance, Genesis itself functions as the "prologue" to the Mosaic Covenant.  Gen. Gen.1-2:3 is a "kingdom preamble" to then the historical prologue that begins at 2:4, and the first of 10 "histories" which describe God's activity of sovereign election and his grace as manifest through the 5 elect lines in contrast to the 5 non-elect lines.  This "history" of election to the glory of God's sovereign grace function as the prologue then to the Sinai Covenant and it's corresponding  stipulations with promised blessings an curses attached to them. This Sinai covenant will in turn function as the historical prologue to the New Covenant even as it will BOTH Typologize the legal aspect of the creation covenant as expected of the first Adam (thus a covenant that looks back in its typology) AND typologize the grace aspect as promised to Adam after the fall with the initiation of the vicarious sacrificial concept,  as again ratified by the vicarious sacrificial concept to Abraham (Chapt. 15), as of course manifest in a full blown way under the Sinai terms and then pointing forward to the ultimate sacrifice of Christ.  All together then, the Bible is one big "covenant" history beginning with the creation covenant and culminating with the new creation as accomplished by Christ as the second Adam! \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li810\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin810\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 And of course, this Old Testament "covenant" context anticipates the New Covenant Context as recorded by Jeremiah. \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Jer. 31:31}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   The days are surely coming, says the LORD, when I will make a }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 new covenant}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  with the house of Israel and the house of Judah.  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 32}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  It will not be like the covenant that I made with their ancestors when I took them by the hand to bring them out of the land of Egypt--a covenant that they broke, though I was their husband, says the LORD.  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 33}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  But this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, says the LORD: I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. \par }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Jer. 32:40}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  I will make }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 an everlasting covenant}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  with them, never to draw back from doing good to them; and I will put the fear of me in their hearts, so that they may not turn from me.}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\fs22\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Clearly then, the concept of "covenant" is the Bible's way of understanding itself and the revelation that is granted by it.  Even our religious "rites" in worship are derived from this notion of "covenant.    E.g. The  word "berith" is from the verb form "to cut" which is derived from the ceremony used to describe covenant makings in the ancient days. The curse element of the covenant treaty was graphically depicted by the "cutting" of animals whose life supporting blood would be expelled from its body.  This  imagery is first revealed when God clothed Adam and Eve by the skins of a slain animal.  The "flaming swords" also get at this cutting aspect depicting the covenant curse as used to guard the way back to the garden of Eden and later placed on the curtains that guarded the way into the Holy of Holies in the Tabernacle.   As it will be shown later in greater detail, the cutting imagery will play out in many different covenant rites-- including of course the rites of sacrifice/Lord's Table and circumcision/baptism. (For  as  "berith" is assigned to the waters of Noah's day as depicting God's covenant curse, so then baptism will be related to these waters as it is also related to circumcision in the New Testament--  see below)  \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 iii.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-180\li1080\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl2\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The Basic Form of a Covenant (Treaty) \par }\pard \ql \li900\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 a. Preamble: Name, title, etc-- to motivate fear and respect... "I am..." (Dt. 1:1-5)\par b. Historical Prologue:  What the sovereign has done to motivate love, respect, etc.   (Dt. 1:6-4:49) \par c. Stipulations: commitments  from vassals, both general and specific (Dt. 5-26)\par d. Sanctions: Curses and Blessing or Covenant Ratification as qualified by the breaking or keeping of the covenant (Dt. 27-30)\par e. Document Clause and Witness: what to do with the treaty document-- where and when to read it, who witnesses the oaths made\u8230\'c9 (Dt. 31-34) \par f. Oath of Ratification and \ldblquote Cutting\rdblquote  (Berith)  Ceremony.. those the word \ldblquote covenant\rdblquote  derived from the word \ldblquote to cut\rdblquote \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs18\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 iv.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-180\li1080\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl2\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Two Types of Covenants??  \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl3\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Covenant of Works }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (WCF 7.2) }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote Pre-Redemptive\rdblquote }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The first covenant made with man was a covenant of works, wherein life was promised to Adam; and in him to his posterity, upon condition of perfect and personal obedience.\par \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 2.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl3\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Covenant of Grace }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (WCF 7.3-4) }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote Redemptive\rdblquote }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Man, by his fall, having made himself incapable of life by that covenant, the Lord was pleased to make a second, commonly called the covenant of grace; wherein He freely offereth unto sinners life and salvation by Jesus Christ; requiring of them faith in Him, that they may be saved, and promising to give unto all those that are ordained unto eternal life His Holy Spirit, to make them willing, and able to believe.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 3.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl3\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Perhaps better, \ldblquote Promise of Grace\rdblquote  which then maintains the importance of \ldblquote works/obedience in gaining the blessings of the covenant}{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \endash e.g. }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 It is called by Paul a "promise" rather than a "covenant" in Gal.3:18 as related to God\rquote s taking upon himself the obligations of the eternal treaty on behalf of humanity in the work of Christ! \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Note Kline: \par }\pard \qj \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "}{\b\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The difference between the pre-redemptive and redemptive covenant is not then that the latter substitutes promise for law.}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The difference is rather that redemptive covenant adds promise to law.}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Redemptive covenant is simultaneously a promise administration of guaranteed blessings and a law administration of blessing dependent on obedience, with the latter foundational.  The weakness of the traditional designation, "Covenant of Works" for the pre-redemptive covenant is that it fails to take account of the }{\i\f25\fs20\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 continuity of the law principle }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 in redemptive revelation and therefore is not a sufficiently distinctive term.  The principles of "works" continues into redemptive covenant administration, not only in the sense already stressed that the blessings of redemption are secured by the works of a federal head who must satisfy the law's demands, but, in the sense, too, that none of the many represented by Christ attains to the promised consummation of the covenant's beatitude except he attains to that holiness without which man does not see God." (p.13-14)... "Coherence can be achieved in Covenant Theology only by the subordination of grace to law."}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  (p.17)  (Meredith Kline, \ldblquote Law Covenant\rdblquote ) \par \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 4.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls33\ilvl3\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Key Interpretative Observation:  Depending on who \ldblquote swears,\rdblquote  the covenant is \ldblquote gracious-righteousness\rdblquote  vs. \ldblquote works-righteousness.\rdblquote  \par }\pard \qj \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "Every divine-human covenant in Scripture involves a }{\i\f25\fs20\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 sanction-sealed commitment}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  to maintain a particular relationship or follow a stipulated course of action.  In general then a covenant may be defined as a relationship under sanctions." (p.3)... "}{\i\f25\fs20\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 It is this swearing of the ratificatory oath that provides an identification mark by which we can readily distinguish in the divine covenants of Scripture between a law covenant and one of promise.}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   For it is evident that if God swears the oath of the ratification ceremony, that particular covenantal transaction is one of promise, whereas if man is summoned to swear the oath, the particular covenant thus ratified is one of law. " (p.3-4)   }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (p.5)  Meredith Kline, \ldblquote Law Covenant\rdblquote ) \par }\pard \qj \li180\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin180\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \qj \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Compare then the Abrahamic Promise in Gen. 15 where oath is taken by God (flaming torch) vs. where oath is taken by people (Ex. 19) Note also the oath of man in Gen. 2:23ff vs. the promise oath of God in Gen. 3. 14ff)\par }\pard \qj \li180\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin180\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 C.f Paul\rquote s discussion in Galatians\emdash  Promise is added to law and can\rquote t then be annulled by Law...\par }\pard \ql \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 3:15-17}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Brothers and sisters, I give an example from daily life: once a person's will has been ratified, no one adds to it or annuls it.  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 16}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Now the promises were made to Abraham and to his offspring; it does not say, \ldblquote And to offsprings,\rdblquote  as of many; but it says, \ldblquote And to your offspring,\rdblquote  that is, to one person, who is Christ.  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 17}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  My point is this: the law, which came four hundred thirty years later, does not annul a covenant previously ratified by God, so as to nullify the promise.  }{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 18}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  For if the inheritance comes from the law, it no longer comes from the promise; but God granted it to Abraham through the promise.}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Summary:}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The Subsequent Need for a "Covenant" Hermeneutic}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1620\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx1080\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1620\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote Everyone agrees that all interpretation of scripture should be sensitive to the context in which a given passage of scripture appears.  Our point is that every passage of scripture falls into some }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 covenantal}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  context as well as some historical or cultural or linguistic context.  Thus, the "meaning" of any passage of scripture is conditioned by our overall understanding of the various covenants and their relations to one another.  All of His laws are given in some covenantal context, and that context must be considered as part of the interpreter's duty. (By way of an example)  The interpreter must always ask, }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 In what ways, if any, are the peculiar features of a given covenant-administration reflected in this particular law?  }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 If we ask this of Leviticus 20:11, we answer: the covenant people in the Sinai administration were required to wield the sword, exercising civil/judicial authority to punish certain crimes capitally.  The covenant people in the New Covenant administration are neither required nor permitted (institutional as the people of God-- the church) to wield the sword in such a manner.  (John 18) On the other hand, the New Covenant continues to teach that sexual immorality is sin; this is not peculiar to the Sinai administration.  Yet we should discipline church members who are sexually immoral, but we should not execute them.\rdblquote \par }\pard \qr \li1620\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx1080\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1620\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 T. David Gordon\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Therefore, Determined by the principle of historic progression, a "biblical theology" seeks to understand the course of revelation as revealed in distinct redemptive periods determined in strict agreement with the lines of cleavage drawn by revelation itself. It should be noted that redemptive history is not the same as covenant history-- in that various and significant epochs of redemptive history might all take place within one covenant epoch-- yet redemptive history is the story of God's redemption as revealed through covenant makings.  Therefore, redemptive-history will necessarily require an analysis of covenant and the way covenant makings play out in a "word-event-word" pattern as determined by a particular covenant context.  So for instance, the interpreter of scripture will want to consider the relation of "creation covenant" and the successive "covenant makings" which mark progressive periods of revelation.  In what way are the successive covenants related to the creation covenant?  How does the legal aspect of the creation covenant play out in redemptive history?  How does the grace aspect get added to the legal aspect-- to annul the legal aspect or to fulfill the legal aspect?  These and many other such question are the kinds of things that will provide a lens through which to interpret the various narratives, teachings, wisdom sayings and prophecies that are contained in holy scripture!\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 II. Covenantal Methodology  \par 1.  A Necessary Reorientation \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1. The entire counsel of God's word (must take the whole story into account)\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The pervasive meaning of Scripture should be brought to bear on any single portion.  Biblical revelation is self-elucidating because it has an organic, unified structure. (R. Gaffin, p.xviii)\par \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2.  God's revelation is the interpretation of redemption history.  \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote God's word invariably has his redemptive deeds for its subject matter.  Indeed, apart from redemption revelation has nothing to say, \lquote it would be suspended in the air.\rquote   Revelation is a function of redemption.\rdblquote   (p.xvi, quote from Biblical Theology,p.24)\par \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2. God's revelation unfolds over time through successive "covenant" makings that reach a climax and fulfillment in Jesus. \par \par 3. Different stages account for different styles and circumstances that must be taken into account. \par \par 4. We must distinguish between the uniquenesses and ultimate similiarities between covenants.  \par \par 5. Diverse contexts produce one wholistic  "dogma" such that we don't believe a diversity of things about a single topic (thus the value of systematic theology)}{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2. Methodological Stages in Biblical Theology: \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "The burden of biblical theology is to orient biblical interpretation to the history of redemption in a pointed and programmatic fashion.  Revelation has its structure and serves its (undeniably multiple and diverse) functions as the progressive attestation and interpretation of the ongoing work of redemption.  Any theological reflection basing itself on biblical interpretation must recognize and work from out of this redemptive-historical framework." (Gaffin, p.xx)\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \fi-180\li900\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1. Step One:}{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   To  relate the text to its immediate covenantal context.   \par 2. Step Two: To understand the text in light of God's total revelation especially as ultimately revealed in the New Covenant.  \par \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 3. Advantages: \par }\pard \ql \fi-180\li900\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin900\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1. Guards against moralizing certain passages through the use of false analogies between the biblical narrative and contemporary life even to the detriment of the fuller theological significance. \par  \par 2. Guards against a theology of "proof-texting."\par  \par 3. Can recognize both the significance of certain passages within its own redemptive context and in the present redemptive context whereby the biblical-theological understanding of scripture is made relevant to us.\par \par 4. Guards against emotionalism and subjectivistic interpretations whereby the normative (governing) value of Biblical revelation is retained to the interpreter in any practical sense.  (We are still the listeners.)\par \par 5. Provides a proper hermeneutic from which to derived "biblical ethics."}{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Summary:  }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 A redemptive historical orientation is not some kind of dispensable exegetical luxury.  At stake is nothing less than the right way of interpreting Scripture.  At issue here is simply the fundamental principle that the test is to be interpreted in the light of its context.  In the case of Scripture, the redemptive-historical structure or framework established by Scripture itself is the contextual factor having the broadest bearing on a given text.}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \tab (Gaffin, p.xxii)}{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 II. Covenant Through Redemptive History Explained (The Principle of Continuity and Discontinuity Explained)\par \par {\listtext\pard\plain\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls36\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The \ldblquote Big Picture\rdblquote   (c.f. Review Diagrams in Addendum, \ldblquote A Summary of the Covenants by Brian Lee) }{\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls16\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Creation Covenant-- Informed by the principle of works between God and humanity of which humanity failed. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls16\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Redemption Covenant -- the eternal agreement between the Father and the Son of the Holy Trinity in order to save the elect from humanity, still informed by the principle of works, albeit the works of Christ on behalf of the elect. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls16\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Abrahamic Covenant-- The promise of Grace initially given to Adam after the fall is predicated upon the redemption covenant between the Father and the Son as was ratified explicitly in the Abrahamic covenant bases upon justification by faith.  The gracious principle is emphasized as ultimately related to the promise given to Abraham concerning a multi-national, multi-ethnic redeemed community as was recorded in Genesis 15. Here, it is God himself who is bound by oath to fulfill his promise.  And yet, the fulfillment of the Abrahamic covenant would be both typoligical as in the land given to Israel, but ultimately in the heavenly inheritance given to all true believers. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls16\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Typolical Covenants meant to typify (foreshadow) the redemption covenant that would be accomplished by Christ whereby, as informed by the principle of works, temporal blessings and curses are predicated upon various "works" stipulations.  \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 1.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls17\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\faauto\ls17\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Noahic Covenant-- although Noah was saved by grace through faith-- his temporal salvation was conditioned upon his obedience. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 2.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls17\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\faauto\ls17\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Mosaic Covenant-- again, although Moses was saved by grace  as was typified by the temple/sacrificial system itself, the nations of Israel, as a geo-political theocracy, was either blessed or cursed as a nation based upon works.  The Mosaic context was ethnic specific as related to temporal prosperity or curse based on works (note Israel's pledge of performance in Ex.24:7). \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls18\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Telos}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Fulfillment in Christ-- Christ  fulfills the ultimate purpose of all previous typological covenants in continuity with Abraham albeit mediated through new administrative means of grace( see ordinance chart).  The Church becomes the "new Israel" (see }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The Israel of God in Prophecy, }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 LaRondelle).  The royal, priestly nation is not multi-ethnic as spiritual vs. temporal, albeit visibly manifest by the three defining marks of the church in her prophetic, priestly and kingly aspects in the present age.  \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls18\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The now/not yet completion of redemption whereby in the present age, the kingdom of God has been inaugurated and completely secured by the work of Christ, but it is not yet fully completed in that we live in the "last days" as the church under discipline/humiliation awaiting her final exaltation/glorification when Christ returns. I.e. We are presently living within tribulation out of which is built the kingdom of God.  \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Important Observations Summarized: (c.f. diagrams) \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\faauto\ls36\ilvl1\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Covenant of Creation and the \ldblquote Works\rdblquote  Principle: (see earlier)\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\faauto\ls36\ilvl1\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Subsequent Covenants of Work Typologized in TEMPORAL (Geo/political) terms.  \par {\listtext\pard\plain\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 i.\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-180\li2160\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx2160\faauto\ls36\ilvl2\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Noatic\par {\listtext\pard\plain\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af25\dbch\af0\loch\f25 ii.\tab}Mosaic\par }\pard \ql \li1980\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1980\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440\faauto\ls36\ilvl1\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0