Patriarchial "place" at Bethel: \par }\pard \ql \li1170\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1170\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "Bethel" means " house of God!  Bethel was in short the patriarchial "place" for covenant renewal which anticipates the temple under Mosaic covenant and even our Lord's Table which today  mediates the presence of God.  The "place" was where God in a special way spoke to the patriarchs and where an sacrifical offering was made at an altar.  This place was clearly anticipating the tabernacle under the Sinai covenant.  Bethel was the place that God spoke to Abraham. \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 12:8}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par From there he moved on to the hill country on the east of Bethel, and pitched his tent, with Bethel on the west and Ai on the east; and there he built an altar to the LORD and invoked the name of the LORD. \par }\pard \ql \li1170\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1170\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\tx576\tx864\tx1152\tx1440\tx1728\tx2016\tx2304\tx2592\tx2880\tx3168\tx3456\tx3744\tx4032\tx4320\tx4608\tx4896\tx5184\tx5472\tx5760\tx6048\tx6336\tx6624\tx6912\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 In Gen.28:16-17, Jacob makes this comment with respect to Bethel: \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\tx576\tx864\tx1152\tx1440\tx1728\tx2016\tx2304\tx2592\tx2880\tx3168\tx3456\tx3744\tx4032\tx4320\tx4608\tx4896\tx5184\tx5472\tx5760\tx6048\tx6336\tx6624\tx6912\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote Surely the LORD is in }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 this place}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 --and I did not know it!\rdblquote   }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 17}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  And he was afraid, and said, \ldblquote How awesome is t}{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 his place}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ! This is none other than the }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 house of God, and this is the gate of heaven.\rdblquote \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\tx288\tx576\tx864\tx1152\tx1440\tx1728\tx2016\tx2304\tx2592\tx2880\tx3168\tx3456\tx3744\tx4032\tx4320\tx4608\tx4896\tx5184\tx5472\tx5760\tx6048\tx6336\tx6624\tx6912\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\tx576\tx864\tx1152\tx1440\tx1728\tx2016\tx2304\tx2592\tx2880\tx3168\tx3456\tx3744\tx4032\tx4320\tx4608\tx4896\tx5184\tx5472\tx5760\tx6048\tx6336\tx6624\tx6912\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Significance here is the recognition that God had consecrated that "place" as a place of covenant renewal-- thus here we see God's initiation of renewed promises based on his prior action and soverign election-- we expect then a reciprocation of worship as always with repentance/faith, vows and dedication  Later, God would again reveal himself and with explicit reference to Bethel. \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par Gen. 31:13}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par I am the God of Bethel, where you anointed a pillar and made a vow to me. Now leave this land at once and return to the land of your birth.\rquote \rdblquote  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 God also command Jacob to go to Bethel for covenant renewal. \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par Gen. 35:1}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \u182\'a6 God said to Jacob, \ldblquote Arise, go up to Bethel, and settle there. Make an altar there to the God who appeared to you when you fled from your brother Esau.\rdblquote  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 35:3}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  then come, let us go up to Bethel, that I may make an altar there to the God who answered me in the day of my distress and has been with me wherever I have gone.\rdblquote  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 35:6}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Jacob came to Luz}{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (that is, Bethel), which is in the land of Canaan, he and all the people who were with him,  }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 7}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  and there he built an altar and called the place El-bethel, because it was there that God had revealed himself to him when he fled from his brother.  }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 8}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  And Deborah, Rebekah's nurse, died, and she was buried under an oak below Bethel. So it was called Allon-bacuth. \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 35:15}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  So Jacob called the place where God had spoken with him Bethel. \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 35:16}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \u182\'a6 Then they journeyed from Bethel; and when they were still some distance from Ephrath, Rachel was in childbirth, and she had hard labor.\par }\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 d.  The Sinai Place-- Tabernacle of God\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The language of "tabernacle" is used over 280 times in the Sinai Covenant.  There, God met with the covenant people through the means of Old Covenant sacraments.  The language of "temple" is used over 300 times.  And no one disputes that this was the language of God's meeting or "dwelling" with his people.  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ex. 40:34}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Then the cloud covered the tabernacle of meeting, and the glory of the Lord filled the tabernacle.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 e. The New Covenant Place-- The Church in its Gathered worship\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Paul clearly understand the New Covenant church to be in succession to the Old Covenant temple of God.. He states in Eph. 2:21, in the context of desscribing the "church" as the "household of God" that it consist of }{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 those  in whom the whole building, being joined together, grows into a holy}{\b\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  in the Lord}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 in whom you also are being built together for a dwelling place of God in the Spirit.  }{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Also, in 1 Cor. 3 and 6, Paul makes reference to the church as the "body of Christ" (as also in Eph.) and says this }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1Cor. 3:16-17}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Do you not know that you are the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of God and that the Spirit of God dwells in you? If anyone defiles the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of God, God will destroy him. For the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of God is holy, which}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  you are. \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 1Cor. 6:19}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Or do you not know that your body is the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God, and you are not your own?\par \par }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Some have misunderstood this reference to be to the individual Christian.  But this is clearly not the case if but by comparison with the language in Ephesians.  Yet also, the earlier admonisions concerning sexual immorality was clearly related to the corporate gathering of the church at the Lord's table, of which the immoral person was to be "excommunicated" in order not to defile the body of Christ.  (1 Cor. 5) \par \par So where is the "temple" in the New Covenant?  As noted by Richard Hays, when Paul speaks of presenting your bodies (plural) as a living sacrifice\u8230\'c9 it is the corporate order that is to be the successor to Israel that Paul has in mind. Or when Paul speaks of God's building (1 Cor.3:10) for instance, he says it is a place where God }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 dwells  }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 (as was the description of the OT temple/tabernacle).  "Do you not know," he asks, "that you (plural) are God's temple and that God's Spirit dwells in you (plural)?" (3:16) As Hayes notes, "to read this last sentence as though it spoke of the Spirit dwelling in the body of the individual Christian would be to miss the force of Paul's audacious metaphor: the apostolically founded community takes the place of the Jerusalem temple as the place where the glory of God resides."\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 f. The Heavenly Tabernacle-- Heaven. \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 15:5}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  After these things I looked, and behold, the temple of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  tabernacle}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of the testimony in heaven was opened. \par }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 21:3}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  And I heard a loud voice from heaven saying,  "Behold, the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  tabernacle}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of God is with men, and He will dwell with them, and they shall be His people. God Himself will be with them and be their God.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 3:12}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   "He who overcomes, I will make him a pillar in the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of My God, and he shall go out no more. And I will write on him the name of My God and the name of the city of My God, the New Jerusalem, which comes down out of heaven from My God. And I will write on him My new name. \par }{\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 14:17}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Then another angel came out of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  which is in heaven, he also having a sharp sickle. \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\ul\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 15:5}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  After these things I looked, and behold, the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  temple}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened.}{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par IV. Covenantal Ethics: \par }{\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\listtext\pard\plain\f2\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \hich\af2\dbch\af0\loch\f2 o\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\faauto\ls42\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Will Avoid BOTH Antinomianism and Legalism\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 This schematic avoids anti-nomianism (licentiousness with respect to living a Godly life as revealed to us by the law that has been perfected by Christ and re-instated under the New Covenant)  on the one hand and legalism on the other (seeking to satisfy the righteous standards of God revealed in the covenant of creation and more thoroughly represented in the Mosaic covenant on our own rather than vicariously through Christ).  This is clearly illustrated by Paul in Galations for instance. \par \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Against Legalism: The "law" was good in so far as it kept the "works" principle of the creation covenant in tact as relative to the covenant stipulations which then drives us to a substitute "law" keeper in the person of Christ.  I.e. Not so as to annul the "works/legal" aspect of the covenant, but as to drive us to that only way whereby the standards of the law might be satisfied.  The grace promise (As Paul refers to it as "promise" not "covenant" is added to the "works covenant" so as to satisfy God's covenant/legal requirements in order to receive the covenant blessings) \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 2:16}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par yet we know that a person is justified not by the works of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  but through faith in Jesus Christ. And we have come to believe in Christ Jesus, so that we might be justified by faith in Christ, and not by doing the works of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , because no one will be justified by the works of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 . \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 2:19}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par For through the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  I died to the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , so that I might live to God. I have been crucified with Christ; \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 2:21}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par I do not nullify the grace of God; for if justification comes through the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , then Christ died for nothing. \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 3:2}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par The only thing I want to learn from you is this: Did you receive the Spirit by doing the works of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  or by believing what you heard? \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 3:5}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Well then, does God supply you with the Spirit and work miracles among you by your doing the works of the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , or by your believing what you heard?}{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\ul\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Against Antinomianism}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 : Even as we are no longer "under the law" in so far as by faith we have embraced Christ to be our covenant keeper on our behalf in order to have the covenant blessings, in so far as the "law" is representing the eternal character of God and we are then being renewed more and more to the imitation of Christ, we are still then exhorted to live a moral life as revealed through the law. \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 5:14}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par For the whole}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  is summed up in a single commandment, \ldblquote You shall love your neighbor as yourself.\rdblquote  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 5:18}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par But if you are led by the Spirit, you are not subject to the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 . \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 5:23}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par gentleness, and self-control. There is no}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  against such things. \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gal. 6:2}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Bear one another's burdens, and in this way you will fulfill the}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  law}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  of Christ.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The above schematic therefore understands that the law as regarding the works principle continues even to this day-- but such as to not be the basis of our salvation but as ratified through the Christ and the apostolic foundation remain as a standard by which we live.   Therefore, true faith will evidence itself in "good works" even as we declare that one is saved by grace through faith.  We see "law" as working itself out both as for an ethical standard and toward our justification in Christ.  \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 b. A Covenant Conditioned Ethics: \par So then, with respect to ethics, we see both continuity and discontinuity within a covenantal framework. Again, T. David Gordon has worked it out this way.  (From unpublished lecture notes) \par \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Continuity: \par }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 While many other circumstances change, in a most comprehensive manner, the human situation is changeless, in the sense that all human existence is created existence; it has always been true, and always will be, that \ldblquote in him we live, and move, and have our being\rdblquote  (Acts 17:28).  The creature\rquote s fundamental ethical circumstance, then, is unchanging:  the creature is obliged to fulfil the purposes of his created existence, giving an account to the Creator (Heb. 4:13   \ldblquote And before him no creature is hidden, but all are naked and laid bare to the eyes of the one to whom we must render an account\rdblquote ).  Thus, the human\rquote s original creational purpose remains his highest goal, regardless of other changes which take place due to the fall and the progress of redemption.  As Cornelius Van Til has put it:\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri540\sb240\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin540\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The ethical ideal that man, as originally created, naturally had to set for himself was }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 the ideal that God wanted him to set for himself}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 .  This is involved in the fact that man is a creature made in the image of God.  God himself is naturally the end of all of man\rquote s activity.  Man\rquote s whole personality was to be a manifestation and revelation on a finite scale of the personality of God.  \par The expressed will of God for man is simply that man shall reflect the moral glory of God on a finite scale.  This is what God asked of man in paradise.  This is what God asked of man in the Old Testament.  This is what God asks of man in the New Testament.  The only difference is that God has himself brought this demand to the consciousness of his people gradually, as they were able to bear it.  (}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Christian Theistic Ethics}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , pp. 41, 150, emphases his).\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\sb240\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Further, this God to whom we must give an account is unchangeable in his moral character.  He is not in process of becoming, but is without any change in his being.  Thus, a second source of the continuity in biblical ethics is that all laws revealed by God, and all values revealed by him, are }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 consistent}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  with his unchanging moral character.  \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Discontinuity in Ethics: \par }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 While the most basic circumstance of the human race (being accountable as creature to the Creator) does not change, many other situational factors do change.  Van Til (}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Christian Theistic Ethics}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , 92-93):\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\sb240\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 They forget that the most orthodox church not only }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 can}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  but }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 must}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , on the basis of its own principle, allow for development.  But it is to be noted that when one allows for this sort of development one is not catering to the evolutionary idea of development.\u8230\'c9Sometimes orthodox men have had no eye for the truly biblical conception of development.  They have been greatly worried sometimes by the externalism of Old Testament ethics, not realizing that externalism is naturally to be expected in a process of historical development.  Even we as Christians may speak of }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 primitive man}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , if only we make clear that we do not mean by that term what the evolutionist means by it.\u8230\'c9Adam was the father of the whole race, and the race as a whole would have to go through a process of development.  At each stage man would have to learn to apply the ethical principles implanted in him by God to the newly arising ethical situation.  It would only be gradually that man would begin to see clearly the most ultimate ethical ideals.\u8230\'c9What we do find in the Old Testament corresponds to what we expect to find.  We actually find that there is a gradual development in the clarity with which the final or ultimate ethical ideal is seen.\par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ethics and the history of redemption. }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \par The great drama of the human race unfolds in the states of innocence, fall, and (partial and entire) redemption.  The duty of the creature undergoes some alteration due to this changing historical circumstance.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\sb240\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls20\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls20\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ethics in the state of innocence: }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imago Dei}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  and }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imitatio Dei.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   There was the comprehensive mandate to cultivate the created order, and there was the prohibition regarding the tree.  In the first, there was fairly evidently an implicit duty for the one made in God\rquote s image to be creative (but not }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ex nihilo}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ) as the Creator was; and to be a governor/ruler as the Creator was (naming).  There is debate, however, as to whether the biblical evidence suggests that edenic existence was informed by legislation.\par }\pard \ql \li2520\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2520\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \ldblquote The view of the paradise state held by the Reformed, sober and yet so sound, emerges at a number of points.  Against the Lutherans and Remonstrants they defended the view that Adam was certainly bound to the moral law, as well as the probation-command.  He was not }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ex lex}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , although he fulfilled it without any compulsion, willingly and out of love.  The moral law was known to Adam by nature and so, unlike the probation-command, did not need to be revealed to him in a special way.  }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 In essence it is identical to the ten commandments but bears another form, for the law given on Sinai presuposes sin}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  and so is almost always expressed negatively:  you shall not, while the moral law before the fall was much more positive.  But just because by the nature of the case the moral law was entirely positive for Adam, it did not make the possibility of sin clear in Adam\rquote s consciousness.  Thus along with the commands there had to come a prohibition, along with the moral law a positive law, along with the commandments whose naturalness and reasonableness Adam recognized, a commandment that in a certain sense was arbitrary and fortuitous.  }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 In the probation-command the entire moral law was staked on a single throw, as it were, for Adam; for him the former incorporated the dilemma:  God or man, God\rquote s authority or his own insight, unconditional obedience or independent investigation, faith or doubt.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   It was a fearsome test that opened the way to eternal blessing or eternal destruction.  [Herman Bavinck:  from }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gereformeerde Dogmatiek}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , provided in private correspondence by Richard B. Gaffin, Jr., 5/20/93, emphases mine].\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\sb240\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls19\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls19\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ethics in the fallen state: }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  The institution of sacrifice, the publication of law, the promise of redemption.  In the fallen state, the creature now must labor under special circumstances.  Bereft of the blessings of the immediate Presence of God, the creature must now labor to fulfil his creaturely mandate.  Further, in his relations to God, sacrifice is now necessitated.  Additionally, though many years later at Sinai, it is necessary for there to be an external code of divinely-sanctioned behaviour, since the expulsion from the garden renders impossible the program of }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imitatio Dei}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  formerly possible.  Adam and his descendants can no longer live in the immediate Presence of God, following his example on a creaturely scale.  Thus, revealed laws (and especially prohibitions) are more necessary (and become far more numerous) than before. 1. Rom. 5:13   sin was indeed in the world before the law, 2. 1Tim. 1:8   3.  Rom. 5:20  4.  Gal. 3:19 \par }\pard \ql \li2520\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2520\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls21\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls21\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ethics in the redeemed state: }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Law continues, yet there is also a revival of the principle of }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imitatio Dei}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  (and }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 imitatio Christi}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 )\par }\pard \ql \li1880\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1880\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 i. External legislation continues:\par }\pard \ql \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rom. 13:8   Owe no one anything, except to love one another; for the one who loves another has fulfilled the law. \u8230\'c910   Love does no wrong to a neighbor; therefore, love is the fulfilling of the law.\par Gal. 6:2   Bear one another's burdens, and in this way you will fulfill the law of Christ. \par Jn. 13:34   I give you a new commandment, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also should love one another.\par 1 Tim. 5:18 For the Scripture says, " You shall not muzzle the ox while he is threshing," and "The laborer is worthy of his wages."  Note that Paul can settle a matter by quoting either OT scripture of sayings of Jesus.  [see Frank Thielman in }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 NTS}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ].\par }\pard \ql \li3240\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin3240\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1800\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 ii. }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imitatio Dei}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   revived\par }\pard \ql \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rom. 8:29   For those whom he foreknew he also predestined to be conformed to the image of his Son, in order that he might be firstborn among  many brethren. \par 1Cor. 11:1   Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ.  \par Eph. 5:1   Therefore be imitators of God, as beloved children, \par Mt. 5:44   But I say to you, Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you,   45 so that you may be children of your Father in heaven; for he makes his sun rise on the evil and on the good, and sends rain on the righteous and on the unrighteous.  \par Matt. 5:48  Therefore you are to be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect.\par Jn. 13:34   I give you a new commandment, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also should love one another.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li1800\ri0\sb240\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls22\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls22\adjustright\rin0\lin1800\itap0\pararsid742525 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ethics in the glorified state}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 --}{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Imitatio Dei}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  in consummated form. (1John 3:2) \par }\pard \ql \li2160\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid742525 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 How will we be like God  We will never be the Creator, never infinite, eternal, or unchangeable.  But we will be holy, uncorrupted in nature, free of sin.  This will take place when we \ldblquote see him as he is.\rdblquote   Why can we not see Him now?  Because, in our corrupted state, no man can see God and live (Ex. 33:20).  Implicit in this clause also, then, is that the corruption, which prohibits us from gazing upon the holiness of God, will be removed at the manifestation of Jesus Christ.  We will be holy, as God is holy; and will once again live in the edenic presence of God.  Note especially the relation between being like God and \ldblquote seeing\rdblquote  Him.  The visible presence of God is sufficient to direct us to \ldblquote be\rdblquote  like Him. (Rom. 8:29)  .  How will be transformed into the \ldblquote image\rdblquote  of his Son?  He has already manifested himself in human flesh, and we are already human, so the \ldblquote image\rdblquote  cannot be a reference to our common humanity.  Rather, following the Adamic christology, \ldblquote image\rdblquote  is a term employed for the race in its original state of righteousness and holiness.  The first Adam lost (or had hopelessly corrupted; there is some debate here) the image of God; the last Adam retained it; and we shall, at glorification, also attain it.\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 V. A Covenantal Understanding of blessing and curses  in this life: \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The righteous don't prosper in a worldly sense.  I.e.   }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Is temporal prosperity conditioned upon the obedience of either individuals or nations?  Was such a distinctive feature of the Sinai administration, or is it an enduring principle in the New Covenant era?  Bolton\rquote s entire volume was written to address precisely the questions of obedience and the consequences thereof, temporal and eternal.  He said this, for instance:  \ldblquote David might well say that he never saw the righteous forsaken, nor their seed begging bread, for outward mercies were the condition annexed to their obedience and God\rquote s part in the covenant, and these failed not to them that obeyed.  But whatever it was then, it is not so now.  Those who are willing and obedient do not now eat the good of the land.  Indeed, it may be that they are in the greatest outward trouble and necessity, whereas they who do wickedly prosper\rdblquote   (}{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 True Bounds}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 , p. 174).\par }\pard \qj \li1080\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par Bolton (correctly, in my judgment) had the better, and more-pauline theology.  He understood that the relation between obedience and temporal prosperity was }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 positive}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  in the Sinai economy; }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 inverse}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  in the New Covenant economy.  In the New Covenant, the nature of our union with Christ is such that we \ldblquote suffer with him, in order that we may be glorified with him.\rdblquote   It is not exceptional, but normative, within our administration, for the outer man to be \ldblquote wasting away\rdblquote  even as the inner man is being renewed.  \par \par When our tradition teaches, as it so frequently does today, that there is essential continuity between the Sinai administration and the New Covenant administration on the issue of the relation between obedience and temporal prosperity, it places upon the shoulders of saints a burden which neither we nor our fathers could bear (e.g., fearing temporal sanctions as the result of disobedience).  It repudiates, implicitly, the normativity of cross-bearing as an aspect of Christian discipleship.  Implicitly, it repudiates the testimony of the  inspired interpreters of our covenant, who perceived earthly affliction as evidence of divine favor (\ldblquote Whom he loves, he chastens\rdblquote ).  Implicitly, it repudiates the apostolic instruction that Christians who hope in Christ for this life are \ldblquote of all men most to be pitied.\rdblquote   Implicitly, it replaces the light yoke and easy burden of Christ with the heavy yoke and unbearable burden of the Sinai administration.  And implicitly, it joins the chorus of Paul\rquote s Corinthian accusers, who denigrated his apostolic authority on the ground of his many sufferings, which very sufferings the apostle presented as proof to the contrary. \tab \tab \tab T. David Gordon\par }\pard \ql \li1170\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1170\itap0 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par \par }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 VI.  The Missional Trajectory of the Gospel For the Nations: \par }{\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The Great Commission into the New Covenant-- The inclusion of the nations into the City of God\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par 1. The preseveration of the }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 imago dei  }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 by common grace. \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Noahic Covenant-- both special and common grace revealed in co-compatibility though distinct.\par a. Notice the creation mandate reinstituted-- Gen.9:1-7\par b. Notice the "covenant" (berith) made with Noah Gen.9:11,\par \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2. The Abrahamic commission: Gen.17 (see above)\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 18:18  "since Abraham shall surely become a great and mighty nation, and all the nations of the earth shall be blessed in him? \par Gen. 26:4  "And I will make your descendants multiply as the stars of heaven; I will give to your descendants all these lands; and in your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed; \par Gen. 27:29 Let peoples serve you, And nations bow down to you. Be master over your brethren, And let your mother's sons bow down to you. Cursed be everyone who curses you, And blessed be those who bless you'' \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 3. The Mosaic continuity with the commission: "a city on a hill"\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Deut. 15:6  "For the Lord your God will bless you just as He promised you; you shall lend to many nations, but you shall not borrow; you shall reign over many nations, but they shall not reign over you. \par Deut. 28:37  "And you shall become an astonishment, a proverb, and a byword among all nations where the Lord will drive you. \par Deut. 30:1  "Now it shall come to pass, when all these things come upon you, the blessing and the curse which I have set before you, and you call them to mind among all the nations where the Lord your God drives you, \par Deut. 30:3  "that the Lord your God will bring you back from captivity, and have compassion on you, and gather you again from all the nations where the Lord your God has scattered you. \par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 4. Jonah-- the repentance of a nationalistic prophet so as to preach the gospel to the nations. \par }\pard \qj \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Hosea and Amos (contemporaries of Jonah) warned against prophets who where more concerned for Isreal than the glory of God and his redemptive purposes.  \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Amos 7:16, }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 You say, do not prophesy against Israel, do not preach against the house of Isaac...\par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Micah 3: 5, }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Thus says the Lord concerning the prophets who lead my people astray, who cry, "peace" when they have something to eat but declare war agaisnt those who put nothing into their mouths... (}{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 11) }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 its prophets give oracles for money, yet they lean upon the Lord and say, surely the Lord is with us."\par \par False prophets:  Concerned for national interest and national policy-- they were less concerned for the spiritual wellfare and mission of Israel than  the political and even parochial interest of a special interest group-- here, the ethno-political interests of Israel.  \par \par Meanwhile, lurking in the shadows from the North was the ever growing power of Assyria representing an impending threat to Israel's prosperity.  Assyria's most important city was }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Ninevah.  }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Jonah 1:1-2: God's command to Jonah\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 vs. 1: }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 the word of the Lord came to Jonah:Arise, Go and Preach!  }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 So why didn't Jonah go eagerly?\par }\pard \qj \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Jonah 4:2\par }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 "I knew that you are a gracious God and merciful, slow to anger, and abounding in steadfast love, and ready to relent from punishing.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\b\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \qj \li1440\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 I.e. God shows compassion to the evil/troubled city of Ninivah and commands Jonah to preach the gospel to them.  But Jonah's conception of the gospel doesn't include wicked/trouble Ninevah.  As an ardent Israel nationalist, Nivevah is the last place he wants to see God's salvation come.  He flees the community of faith hoping to flee personal responsibility.  Jonah's vision is too small!\par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 5. Prophetic expectations of the Great Commision being realized through a Messiah in the "last days" \par }\pard \ql \li1080\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 {\f25\fs18\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Is. 2:2 Now it shall come to pass in the latter days That the mountain of the Lord's house Shall be established on the top of the mountains, And shall be exalted above the hills; And all nations shall flow to it. \par Is. 5:26 \u182\'a6 He will lift up a banner to the nations from afar, And will whistle to them from the end of the earth; Surely they shall come with speed, swiftly. \par Is. 11:12 He will set up a banner for the nations, And will assemble the outcasts of Israel, And gather together the dispersed of Judah From the four corners of the earth. \par Is. 43:9 Let all the nations be gathered together, And let the people be assembled. Who among them can declare this, And show us former things? Let them bring out their witnesses, that they may be justified; Or let them hear and say,  "It is truth.'' \par Is. 45:1  "Thus says the Lord to His anointed, To Cyrus, whose right hand I have held--To subdue nations before him And loose the armor of kings, To open before him the double doors, So that the gates will not be shut: \par Is. 45:20 \u182\'a6  "Assemble yourselves and come; Draw near together, You who have escaped from the nations. They have no knowledge, Who carry the wood of their carved image, And pray to a god that cannot save. \par Is. 49:22 \u182\'a6 Thus says the Lord God:  "Behold, I will lift My hand in an oath to the nations, And set up My standard for the peoples; They shall bring your sons in their arms, And your daughters shall be carried on their shoulders; \par Is. 52:10 The Lord has made bare His holy arm In the eyes of all the nations; And all the ends of the earth shall see The salvation of our God. \par Is. 52:15 So shall He sprinkle many nations. Kings shall shut their mouths at Him; For what had not been told them they shall see, And what they had not heard they shall consider. \par Is. 54:3 For you shall expand to the right and to the left, And your descendants will inherit the nations, And make the desolate cities inhabited. \par Is. 55:5 Surely you shall call a nation you do not know, And nations who do not know you shall run to you, Because of the Lord your God, And the Holy One of Israel; For He has glorified you.'' \par }{\b\f25\fs18\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Is. 56:7 Even them I will bring to My holy mountain, And make them joyful in My house of prayer. Their burnt offerings and their sacrifices Will be accepted on My altar; For My house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations.'' \par }{\f25\fs18\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Is. 60:12 For the nation and kingdom which will not serve you shall perish, And those nations shall be utterly ruined. \par Is. 61:11 For as the earth brings forth its bud, As the garden causes the things that are sown in it to spring forth, So the Lord God will cause righteousness and praise to spring forth before all the nations. \par Is. 64:2 As fire burns brushwood, As fire causes water to boil--To make Your name known to Your adversaries, That the nations may tremble at Your presence\par Is. 66:18 \u182\'a6  "For I know their works and their thoughts. It shall be that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come and see My glory.  19  "I will set a sign among them; and those among them who escape I will send to the nations: to Tarshish and Pul and Lud, who draw the bow, and Tubal and Javan, to the coastlands afar off who have not heard My fame nor seen My glory. And they shall declare My glory among the Gentiles.  20  "Then they shall bring all your brethren for an offering to the Lord out of all nations, on horses and in chariots and in litters, on mules and on camels, to My holy mountain Jerusalem,'' says the Lord,  "as the children of Israel bring an offering in a clean vessel into the house of the Lord. }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 6. Ministry of Christ: \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Mark 11:17 Then He taught, saying to them,  "Is it not written,  "My house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations'? But you have made it a "den of thieves.' '' \par Matt. 28:19  "Go therefore and make disciples of all the nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, \par Luke 24:47  "and that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in His name to all nations, beginning at Jerusalem. \par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li260\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin260\itap0 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 7. The apostolic ministry to the nations: \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rom. 1:5 Through Him we have received grace and apostleship for obedience to the faith among all nations for His name, \par Rom. 4:17  (as it is written,  "I have made you a father of many nations'') in the presence of Him whom he believed--God, who gives life to the dead and calls those things which do not exist as though they did;  18 who, contrary to hope, in hope believed, so that he became the father of many nations, according to what was spoken,  "So shall your descendants be.'' \par Rom. 16:26 but now has been made manifest, and by the prophetic Scriptures has been made known to all nations, according to the commandment of the everlasting God, for obedience to the faith-- \par Gal. 3:8 And the Scripture, foreseeing that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, preached the gospel to Abraham beforehand, saying,  "In you all the nations shall be blessed.'' \par \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 7. The ultimate consumation of the Great commission: \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Rev. 2:26  "And he who overcomes, and keeps My works until the end, to him I will give power over the nations-- \par Rev. 7:9 \u182\'a6 After these things I looked, and behold, a great multitude which no one could number, of all nations, tribes, peoples, and tongues, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, with palm branches in their hands, \par Rev. 10:11 And he said to me,  "You must prophesy again about many peoples, nations, tongues, and kings.'' \par Rev. 11:9 Then those from the peoples, tribes, tongues, and nations will see their dead bodies three-and-a-half days, and not allow their dead bodies to be put into graves. \par Rev. 11:18 The nations were angry, and Your wrath has come, And the time of the dead, that they should be judged, And that You should reward Your servants the prophets and the saints, And those who fear Your name, small and great, And should destroy those who destroy the earth.'' \par Rev. 12:5 She bore a male Child who was to rule all nations with a rod of iron. And her Child was caught up to God and His throne. \par Rev. 21:24 And the nations of those who are saved shall walk in its light, and the kings of the earth bring their glory and honor into it. \par Rev. 21:26 And they shall bring the glory and the honor of the nations into it.}{\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par \page }{\b\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Addendum: }{\b\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Distinguishing the Temporal From The Eternal  }{\b\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 in Gen 15 and 17\par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par So here is the issue-- the Mosaic covenant, in so far as it added to the Abrahamic covenant, was a }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 temporal}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  covenant.  Such things as "land" and "ethnic nation" are the kinds of blessings promised to Israel if they were to keep the covenant.  (Therefore, the temporal aspects of the curse attached to the stipulations where such things as banishment from the land and poverty whereas the blessings attached to the stipulations were settlement in the land and great wealth.  Paul says this was to function as a guardian until the time was right for the fuller fulfillment. This }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 temporal}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  aspect of the Sinai covenant was typological in two directions and representing both the works/legal aspect of the creation covenant and the grace promise attached to the creation covenant after the fall.  E.g. It represented the works principle of the creation covenant in order to drive us to the grave promise attached to the creation covenant after the fall. Yet in so far as the Mosaic covenant didn't annul the Abrahamic covenant as related to the promise of grace, it wouldn't be right to contrast the Mosaic covenant with grace either.  The continuation of the "grace" rites as through circumcision and sacrifices, although typological themselves, directed the people }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 back}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  to the grace promise as woven through Genesis and of course }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 forward}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  to the grace promise as revealed in Christ.  Once Christ has satisfied the grace promise as revealed by these grace "rites", the church foundation includes new "rites" that are viewed in succession to these older rites so as to reflect the finished work of Christ.  (Thus, the passover/sacrifice rites that is replaced by the Lord's supper depicting Christ's blood, the circumcision rites that is replaced by baptism depicting Christ's washing related to effectual calling by the Holy Spirit. (see below) E.g. there needs to be a way to distinguish the succession of the grace principle as related somehow to the works principle.  \par \par This schematic best explains what one reads in Genesis. For it is clear that the works principle is functioning in so far as it is related to acquisition of land and geo-political nation, even as God acts after his sovereign grace in electing some and not others into the covenant history.  There is in short BOTH a "works" principle and a "grace" principle working itself out with Abraham-- the former relative to the acquisition of temporal benefits and the latter relative to the acquisition of eternal benefits through divine election.  Take for example Genesis 17. \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\b\f25\fs18\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 17:1}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  When Abram was ninety-nine years old, the LORD appeared to Abram, and said to him, \ldblquote I am God Almighty; walk before me, and be blameless.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 2}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  And I will make my covenant between me and you, and will make you exceedingly numerous.\rdblquote }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par Gen. 17:9}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \u182\'a6 God said to Abraham, \ldblquote As for you, you shall keep my covenant, you and your offspring after you throughout their generations.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 10}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  This is my covenant, which you shall keep, between me and you and your offspring after you: Every male among you shall be circumcised.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 11}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  You shall circumcise the flesh of your foreskins, and it shall be a sign of the covenant between me and you.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 M. Kline: \par Abraham's call, the initial revelation of the covenant, confronted him as a demand, an imperative to which the promises were attached.  It was only as Abraham responded in obedience to the command of God's call that he could enter into the hope offered in the promises.  This is not to say that the promised heavenly inheritance was to be secured by the works principle, that is, on the ground of obedience.  It does mean that the divine promises of the covenant never existed apart from human obligations. \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 So what's going on here?  Evidently, there is one level of fulfillment that is relative to human obedience and another level that is not.  Paul's point is that the Mosaic covenant did not annul that aspect revealed to Abraham (and even Adam before him) after the principle of God's sovereign grace.  Here again, the context is important, for while "human obligation" is required in any covenant by the definition of a covenant itself, God has taken upon himself to secure those human obligations so that those chosen of God might then have access to the covenant promises.  This was the point earlier in Genesis 15.\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\b\f25\fs18\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Gen. 15:7}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Then he said to him, \ldblquote I am the LORD who brought you from Ur of the Chaldeans, to give you this land to possess.\rdblquote   }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 8}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  But he said, \ldblquote O Lord GOD, how am I to know that I shall possess it?\rdblquote   }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 9}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  He said to him, \ldblquote Bring me a heifer three years old, a female goat three years old, a ram three years old, a turtledove, and a young pigeon.\rdblquote   }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 10}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  He brought him all these and cut them in two, laying each half over against the other; but he did not cut the birds in two. }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \u8230\'c9}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 17}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  \u182\'a6When the sun had gone down and it was dark, a smoking fire pot and a flaming torch passed between these pieces.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 18}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  On that day the LORD made a covenant with Abram, saying, \ldblquote To your descendants I give this land, from the river of Egypt to the great river, the river Euphrates\u8230\'c9\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs20\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The smoking firepot and blazing torch are symbols of God, even as he appeared later as a pillar of cloud and a pillar of fire, even as his presence on Mt. Sinai was accompanied by smoke and fire.  So here God himself, after making his promise to Abram, cuts a covenant by walking through the carcasses. And as God walks through the dead animals, it is God who will guarantee the covenant-- it is God who will make the payments, and who then here takes the oath such as to invoke curse upon himself if payments are not made in full-- it is God who will suffer our part-- and it is God who is the sole guarantor of this covenant-- a covenant as initiated to Adam and his descendents through Seth, ratified to Noah and his descendants through Shem, her ratified to Abram as through his descendents, even one born as from his own body!  \par \par So what exactly was God guaranteeing?  IF viewed from the lens of the New Covenant, we see that the "land" is symbolic of "heaven."  \par }\pard\plain \ql \li630\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin630\itap0 \f5\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Heb. 11:8}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   By faith Abraham obeyed when he was called to set out for a place that he was to receive as an inheritance; and he set out, not knowing where he was going.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 9}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  By faith he stayed for a time in the land he had been promised, as in a foreign land, living in tents, as did Isaac and Jacob, who were heirs with him of the same promise.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 10}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  For he looked forward to the city that has foundations, whose architect and builder is God\u8230\'c9}{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 39}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   Yet all these, though they were commended for their faith, did not receive what was promised,  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 40}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  since God had provided something better so that they would not, apart from us, be made perfect.}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Now lets turn look at the Mosaic context.  If we compare the covenant made in Genesis 15 with the covenant made in Exodus 24.   Specifically, \par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li630\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin630\itap0 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 24:4}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  And Moses wrote down all the words of the LORD. He rose early in the morning, and built an altar at the foot of the mountain, and set up twelve pillars, corresponding to the twelve tribes of Israel.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 5}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  He sent young men of the people of Israel, who offered burnt offerings and sacrificed oxen as offerings of well-being to the LORD.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 6}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Moses took half of the blood and put it in basins, and half of the blood he dashed against the altar.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 7}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Then he took the book of the covenant, and read it in the hearing of the people; }{\i\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 and they said, \ldblquote All that the LORD has spoken we will do, and we will be obedient.\rdblquote }{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525   }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 8}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Moses took the blood and dashed it on the people, and said, \ldblquote See the blood of the covenant that the LORD has made with you in accordance with all these words.\rdblquote }{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Whereas in Genesis 15, God takes the vows of obedience, here in Exodus 24, the people take the vows.  The "blood" of sacrifice looks back to the Abraham and even Adam as to God's promise of grace, whereas the vows of the people look to that aspect that is temporal as related to land and ethnic nation.    From the standpoint of the New Covenant then, how does Paul view the Mosaic covenant according to Genesis 4:24ff? \par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 4:24}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Now this is an allegory: these women are two covenants. One woman, in fact, is Hagar, from Mount Sinai, bearing children for slavery.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 25}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  Now Hagar is Mount Sinai in Arabia and corresponds to the present Jerusalem, for she is in slavery with her children.  }{\b\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 26}{\f25\fs20\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  But the other woman corresponds to the Jerusalem above; she is free, and she is our mother.\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\fs18\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\fs22\cf1\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Here then in language quite clear, we have the principle of relating the covenants in clear view.  One, according to Paul corresponds to the earthly "present" Jerusalem.  This one is compared to slavery in so far as its burdens are concerned.  But the other covenant, this corresponds to the "Jerusalem above,"  that is the heavenly Jerusalem that is related to freedom.   As noted by }{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Samuel Bolton about the Mosaic covenant,  \ldblquote Yet\u8230\'c9this covenant of grace was dispensed to the Jews in such a legal manner that it seems to be nothing else but the repetition of the covenant of works\rdblquote .  Samuel Bolton, }{\i\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The True Bounds of Christian Freedom}{\f25\fs22\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  (Edinburgh: Banner of Truth), 90.\par }{\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \ql \li540\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin540\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 Those who propose that there is a legal aspect to the Sinai administration do not deny that there is also a gracious aspect, wherein the promises made to Abraham are repeated. The legal aspect has to do with the land.  Those who believe there is a legal aspect in the Sinai administration do not believe the legal aspect is the condition of  salvation, but the condition of prospering in the earthly land of Canaan.  \par \par The concept is this:  That some of the potential blessings (temporal prosperity in Canaan) of the Sinai administration are conditioned upon the obedience of the Israelites to the stipulations of the covenant; in contrast to the blessings of the Abrahamic administration, that are manifestly conditioned upon God alone, who gives life to the dead, and can give to old Abraham and barren Sarah descendants as numerous as the stars of heaven.  \par \par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 The reason Paul can say the Mosaic covenant ("law") is not characterized by faith (Gal. 3:12), for instance, is not because there is no room for faith in it.  Paul\rquote s comments in Galatians 3 are designed to explain the }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 distinctive}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  features of the Abrahamic and Sinai covenants, }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 comparatively}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  speaking.  Comparatively speaking, the Abrahamic covenant is called, by synecdoche, \ldblquote promise\rdblquote ; comparatively speaking, the Mosaic covenant is called, by a similar synecdoche, \ldblquote law\rdblquote .  Surely, for instance, many Israelites believed, and faith was indeed promoted by Moses.  However, faith was not the condition of prospering in the land of Canaan; obedience to the covenant stipulations was the condition.  Faith, and justification by faith, was already revealed in the Abrahamic administration; insofar as this reality was taught by the officers of the Sinai administration, then, it was not a }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 distinctive}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  feature.  What was }{\i\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 distinctive}{\f25\fs20\insrsid742525\charrsid742525  about Sinai, as compared to the Abrahamic covenant, was the typological nature of the land of Canaan; and obedience to the stipulations of the covenant as the condition of prospering temporally in this earthly land.\par \par }{\b\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }\pard \qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f25\insrsid742525\charrsid742525 \par }}